nest  and potentially had we done a different  time point or provided different types  of nesting material  um a significant difference may  potentially have showed up  and finally we looked at histology  differences between our groups  um and we did find that the facial vein  puncture resulted in the most severe  histologic changes so i'm showing you  here  in a a more zoomed out transverse  section of the head  so the area of the facial vein is  indicated by the arrow and this was in a  mouse that had no significant  um findings then that box and a is sort  of where we're zoomed in in b  and the arrow head is pointing to some  mild sub q hemorrhage  and a facial vein group mouse and then c  and d  are showing you um sort of progressively  more severe hemorrhage and some facial  vein  group mice that are around some other  important structures such as the  submandibular lymph node and salivary  gland  you can actually even see some red blood  cells that are draining into the sinuses  of the lymph node in d um  so we did find more uh incidences and  more severe histologic changes in our  facial vein group  uh we're looking um in e f  at some histo sections from uh tailbeam  incision mice so in e there are no  significant changes and f  we're seeing some mild uh neutrophil  infiltration  and then in g and h i'm showing you  histosections from mice that had the  tail tip amputation phlebotomy performed  in g you can see that caudal vertebra is  still intact and then h you can see that  the caudal vertebra was actually  transected and in these mice we saw  usually mild fibrin maybe mild  neutrophil infiltration  or just sort of a normal response to any  kind of tissue trauma  we did note ideally that the caudal  vertebra would not be transected but  um due to the sort of overall lack of  response  in the behavior and physiologic  parameters that we measured  and the tilted vampiration group and the  lack of any difference in that tail top  amputation group  and the chamtel group we think that  potentially  this impact is not very significant  on the mic so in summary  we suggest that tail temp amputation is  potentially superior phobotomy method  in terms of animal welfare when suitable  for your steady needs  so again in summary we found that there  was no evidence that tilting amputation  caused  more pain or stress to mice than just a  sham manipulation of their tail  or then two already approved methods and  awake adult mice  um we found that facial gain phlebotomy  may actually be the least desirable of  the three methods in terms of animal  welfare  even though it took less total handling  time so again facial being phlebotomy  was associated  with increased audible vocalizations  increased periods of inactivity  reduced speed and open field tests and  more severe  histologic change and we found no  evidence to suggest that providing  analgesia or anesthesia with tail type  amputation  would be beneficial given the lack of  differences between the tail tip  amputation and sham  groups so i'd like to acknowledge my  co-authors that were on this paper  with me as well as some individuals who  were important  for the data analysis stuff and of  course the cornell university center for  animal resources and education  where i conducted the study as part of  my residency training  and i'll put my summary slide back up  and then i would be happy  to take any questions  thank you so much elizabeth for that  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have any questions  you'd like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question drop  down box located on the far  left of your presentation window type  your questions into the box that appear  on the screen and click the send button  we'll answer as many  as questions as we have time for today  so let's get started  let's see what our audience first  questions are  that are already coming in our first  question is  elizabeth how does tail tip amputation  differ from tail tip biopsy that is  commonly  performed for genotyping  i think that's a great question and as i  mentioned before that's sort of one of  the reasons why we are actually  interested  in performing this study so at our  institution and many others  if tail tips are biopsied um to collect  tissue for genotyping  after the mouse has been weaned so we  say after 21 days  they provide or they're required to  provide analgesia under anesthesia  and that's essentially because at that  age in most strains the caudal vertebrae  have fully ossified and we think that  the procedure is more painful that age  than than before 21 days  um and so when we had investigators here  at cornell they were interested in using  the modified tail tip amputation  technique  um we sort of had concerns as to whether  or not they should provide analgesia  and um but there is actually a large  difference in the degree of tissue  that's transected  so you can take usually up to five  millimeters of tail  for um genotyping but we really were  only just taking that caudal even you  know one millimeter one to two  millimeters  um with the tail temp amputation um and  also  usually the genotyping procedures sort  of performed with some kind of  identification so usually the mice are  picked up and they're scruffed  and we also we're performing only this  minimal handling  and minimal tissue removal so that's why  we wanted uh to more evaluate the impact  that this  kind of modified and and minimal tail  tip amputation technique would have  versus  your kind of traditional tissue removal  for for tail biopsy and genotyping  thank you so much and i want to thank  our audience members who are asking  questions today and remind them that any  questions that aren't answered today  elizabeth is more than welcome to answer  via email  we have one more question coming in why  wouldn't providing  analgesia be a safe default when  performing  a phlebotomy i think that is a great  question and sort of i think  often we think you know we're doing good  by by doing that  um and it's sort of our our desire to  just give the analgesia sort of as a  as a default just assuming that  something is painful and that's going to  be  of a benefit but um most of the time  providing analgesia such as giving a  ketoprofen or meloxicam or some other  nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory  is going to be another procedural event  for the mouse so it's an additional  handling procedure it's it's scruffing  the mouse restraining the mouse  and then giving it another needle  injection to actually provide  provide that drug um and actually if  you're going to usually give that drug  long enough to kick in  you would have to do that well enough in  advance of your your blood collection  procedure for those drugs to really be  on board  and then unfortunately that handling  impact and the stress that that caused  the animal may impact your data  collection what you're measuring in your  blood sample  and truly um the benefit provided by  that analgesia  may not outweigh the additional stress  that's caused by the mouse and that  additional handling and  an administration procedure so that kind  of goes back to where i think it's  really important to have these sham only  or handled only groups  in these studies that look at the impact  of phlebotomy  because if you see no difference between  a method and a sham only group  then likely providing analgesia is not  going to improve the welfare and may  actually cause  increased stress because now you've  handled the mice uh twice  wonderful thank you and thank you very  much for your presentation today  and for your important research do you  have any closing remarks you'd like to  share  with the audience before we close today  i just thank you guys for listening i  think that this is a really  important topic that we keep evaluating  especially  now as we get better and better metrics  to be able to evaluate our impact on  mice  so i think that there's still a lot out  there that we can we can learn so i  encourage other people to continue to  conduct these  uh well-designed studies to evaluate the  impacts of phlebotomy on mice that we  can continue to just improve our  techniques  both to improve our our data and also to  have  less impact on animal welfare
 

